<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuroda Yoshitaka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1546">1546</a>–<a href="/years/1604">1604</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Kuroda Kanbei, Kuroda Josui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuroda Yoshitaka, better known as Kuroda Kanbei, was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> and strategist who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in his campaigns to unify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and was rewarded with lands in Buzen province. A convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, he later retired as the lay monk Josui. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> campaign of 1600 he fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a> became the first lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> domain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuroda-yoshitaka.jpg" alt="Kuroda Yoshitaka" width="320" height="704" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshitaka_Kuroda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kuroda Yoshitaka was born at Himeji on 22 December 1546, named Mankichi, the son of Kuroda Mototaka. The family is believed to have come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a>; his grandfather Shigetaka brought it to Himeji as a senior retainer of Kodera Masamoto, the lord of Himeji, and lived at Gochaku Castle. Mototaka was allowed to marry Masamoto's adopted daughter and use the Kodera name. Yoshitaka became head of the family at 21, when his father retired.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1577 he pledged loyalty to the Oda as Hideyoshi led their advance into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chūgoku region</a>, and with Takenaka Hanbei he served as Hideyoshi's strategist against the Mōri clan. In 1578 he went to Arioka Castle to dissuade Araki Murashige from revolting, but Araki imprisoned him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, believing he had defected, sentenced his son Shōjumaru, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a>, to death, but Hanbei saved the boy. Rescued at the Siege of Itami in 1579, Yoshitaka was left lame and blind in one eye.</p><p>He fought at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Battle of Yamazaki</a> in 1582, at Shizugatake in 1583 and Komaki-Nagakute in 1584, and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> forces in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> in 1585. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, under the influence of Takayama Ukon, he was baptised as Dom Simeão and arranged the rescue of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission from Bungo. After Hideyoshi's edict of 1587 against the missionaries he gave up the faith and took the name Josui, perhaps from Josué, the Portuguese form of Joshua.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> he and Nagamasa allied with the Tokugawa general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a>, holding a grudge against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> from the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. In 1600 he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-yoshimune">Ōtomo Yoshimune</a> at the Battle of Ishigakibaru and took part in the Siege of Yanagawa; one theory holds that he meant to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> for himself, briefly holding seven of its provinces. He died in 1604 after Nagamasa had succeeded him, and his grave is at Namazuta in Iizuka. Known as frugal, he took part in building the castles of Himeji, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuroda Yoshitaka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1580 <b>Kuroda Yoshitaka</b> presented the castle to Toyotomi Hideyoshi, who in 1581 expanded it significantly and built a three-storey keep with an area of about 55 square metres.</blockquote><a href="/work/himeji-castle">Himeji Castle · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Kuroda Nagamasa, son of Hideyoshi's strategist <b>Kuroda Kanbei</b>, fought for Tokugawa Ieyasu at Sekigahara and received Chikuzen province in 1600 with about 520,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/fukuoka-domain">Fukuoka Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Himeji, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Fukakusa. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.82,134.69&amp;year=1583" aria-label="Himeji on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kuroda Yoshitaka"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6020 4523h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6020 4523h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.82,134.69&amp;year=1583">What was here in 1583? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
